{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ושלחתה", :translit "v'shilachta", :gloss "and you shall send away"} {:he "לנפשה", :translit "l'nafsha", :gloss "to herself"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "לנפשה", :b "לא בבית אביה הנכרי"}], :frame {:text "Deuteronomy 21:14 addresses the release of a captive woman taken in war: 'you shall send her away to herself (לנפשה).' Ba'al HaTurim reads this phrase as encoding her destination — she is released on her own, not returned to her non-Israelite father's house.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 21:14"]}} {:quotes [{:he "אשר עניתה", :translit "asher 'inita", :gloss "whom you have afflicted"} {:he "לכי תהיין", :translit "lekhi tihyen", :gloss "go, be"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semantic link: the word עניתה (afflicted) in v.14 juxtaposed with לכי תהיין (go, be) in v.13 — proximity teaches the nature of the affliction."}], :frame {:text "In Deuteronomy 21:13–14, the verse states 'go, be (לכי תהיין)' at the end of v.13 and 'whom you have afflicted (אשר עניתה)' at the start of v.14. Ba'al HaTurim uses the syntactic adjacency of these two expressions to interpret what 'afflicting' means in this context: it refers to the act of sexual relations. The Sifrei on this passage addresses the parameters of the captive woman's treatment.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 21:13" "Deuteronomy 21:14"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "לנפשה", :a-value 465, :b "לא בבית אביה הנכרי", :b-value 748, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semantic link: the word עניתה (afflicted) in v.14 juxtaposed with לכי תהיין (go, be) in v.13 — proximity teaches the nature of the affliction.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:37:59.768287883Z", :model "glm-5.2"}